# PATENT CLAIM SET
## Dynamic Directional Transfer Function Updating for Spatial Audio Processing

---

### CLAIM 1 (Method)

A computer-implemented method for spatial audio processing, the method comprising:

(a) obtaining an initial target direction relative to a listener, the initial target direction defining a spatial position of a sound source or a rendering direction for an audio signal;

(b) retrieving, from a memory storing a plurality of directional transfer functions each associated with a respective spatial direction relative to the listener, an initial directional transfer function corresponding to the initial target direction;

(c) processing an input audio signal using the initial directional transfer function to generate a spatially rendered audio output;

(d) tracking, via a sensor system, a change in the target direction relative to the listener over time, the change resulting from at least one of: (i) movement of the listener's head, (ii) movement of the sound source, or (iii) a user-initiated change in rendering direction;

(e) determining, based on the tracked change, an updated target direction;

(f) computing an update criterion based on a magnitude of angular deviation between the initial target direction and the updated target direction relative to a predetermined threshold;

(g) responsive to the update criterion being satisfied, selecting or interpolating an updated directional transfer function corresponding to the updated target direction, wherein the interpolating comprises combining two or more directional transfer functions associated with spatial directions proximate to the updated target direction according to interpolation weights derived from the angular deviation;

(h) applying a smoothing or crossfading operation between the initial directional transfer function and the updated directional transfer function over a transition time interval, so as to reduce audible artifacts in the spatially rendered audio output during the update; and

(i) continuing to process the input audio signal using the updated directional transfer function to generate an updated spatially rendered audio output reflecting the updated target direction,

wherein steps (d) through (i) are repeated dynamically to continuously update the directional transfer function applied to the input audio signal in response to further tracked changes in the target direction.

---

### CLAIM 2 (Dependent — Interpolation Detail)

The method of claim 1, wherein computing the updated directional transfer function comprises:

selecting a set of neighboring directional transfer functions from the plurality of directional transfer functions based on proximity of their associated spatial directions to the updated target direction;

computing interpolation weights based on at least one of: spherical linear interpolation (SLERP) between direction vectors, barycentric coordinates relative to a mesh of measurement directions, or distance-weighted averaging; and

combining the neighboring directional transfer functions, in at least one of a time domain or a frequency domain, according to the computed interpolation weights to produce the updated directional transfer function.

---

### CLAIM 3 (Dependent — Sensor/Tracking Detail)

The method of claim 1, wherein tracking the change in target direction comprises receiving orientation data from at least one of a head-tracking device, an inertial measurement unit, a camera-based tracking system, or a beamforming-based direction-of-arrival estimator, and wherein the updated target direction is determined relative to a listener reference frame derived from the orientation data.

---

### CLAIM 4 (Dependent — Threshold/Rate Limiting)

The method of claim 1, wherein the update criterion further comprises a rate limit constraining a frequency of directional transfer function updates, such that updates are applied only when the angular deviation exceeds the predetermined threshold and a minimum time interval has elapsed since a preceding update, thereby reducing computational load and perceptual artifacts associated with excessively frequent updates.

---

### CLAIM 5 (Dependent — Domain of Processing)

The method of claim 1, wherein the directional transfer functions comprise head-related transfer functions (HRTFs) or head-related impulse responses (HRIRs), and wherein processing the input audio signal comprises convolving the input audio signal with the initial directional transfer function and, subsequently, with the updated directional transfer function, to produce a binaural audio output comprising a left-channel signal and a right-channel signal.

---

### CLAIM 6 (Apparatus)

An apparatus for spatial audio processing, the apparatus comprising:

at least one processor; and

at least one memory storing a plurality of directional transfer functions, each associated with a respective spatial direction relative to a listener, and storing instructions that, when executed by the at least one processor, cause the apparatus to:

obtain an initial target direction relative to the listener;

retrieve an initial directional transfer function corresponding to the initial target direction;

process an input audio signal using the initial directional transfer function to generate a spatially rendered audio output;

receive, from a sensor interface, tracking data indicative of a change in the target direction relative to the listener;

determine an updated target direction based on the tracking data;

evaluate an update criterion based on an angular deviation between the initial target direction and the updated target direction;

responsive to the update criterion being satisfied, compute an updated directional transfer function corresponding to the updated target direction by selecting or interpolating among the plurality of directional transfer functions;

apply a smoothing operation between the initial directional transfer function and the updated directional transfer function over a transition interval; and

process the input audio signal using the updated directional transfer function to generate an updated spatially rendered audio output,

wherein the apparatus is configured to dynamically and repeatedly perform the receiving, determining, evaluating, computing, applying, and processing operations in response to continued tracked changes in the target direction.

---

### CLAIM 7 (Dependent — Apparatus Hardware Detail)

The apparatus of claim 6, further comprising a head-tracking sensor communicatively coupled to the sensor interface, wherein the head-tracking sensor comprises at least one of a gyroscope, an accelerometer, a magnetometer, or an optical tracking module, and wherein the tracking data comprises orientation data expressed in terms of yaw, pitch, and roll relative to a reference orientation.

---

### CLAIM 8 (Dependent — Apparatus Output)

The apparatus of claim 6, further comprising an audio output interface configured to output the spatially rendered audio output and the updated spatially rendered audio output to a pair of headphones or a loudspeaker array, wherein the instructions further cause the apparatus to apply a cross-talk cancellation filter when the audio output interface is configured to output to the loudspeaker array.

---

### CLAIM 9 (Computer Program Product)

A non-transitory computer-readable medium storing computer program instructions that, when executed by at least one processor, cause the at least one processor to perform operations comprising:

obtaining an initial target direction relative to a listener;

retrieving, from a plurality of stored directional transfer functions each associated with a respective spatial direction relative to the listener, an initial directional transfer function corresponding to the initial target direction;

processing an input audio signal using the initial directional transfer function to generate a spatially rendered audio output;

receiving tracking data indicative of a change in the target direction relative to the listener;

determining an updated target direction based on the tracking data;

evaluating an update criterion based on an angular deviation between the initial target direction and the updated target direction relative to a predetermined threshold;

responsive to the update criterion being satisfied, computing an updated directional transfer function corresponding to the updated target direction by selecting or interpolating among the plurality of stored directional transfer functions;

applying a smoothing operation between the initial directional transfer function and the updated directional transfer function over a transition interval; and

processing the input audio signal using the updated directional transfer function to generate an updated spatially rendered audio output,

wherein the operations are repeated dynamically to continuously update the directional transfer function applied to the input audio signal in response to further changes in the target direction indicated by subsequently received tracking data.

---

### CLAIM 10 (Dependent — Program Product, Machine Learning Variant)

The non-transitory computer-readable medium of claim 9, wherein computing the updated directional transfer function comprises applying a trained machine learning model configured to receive the updated target direction, or a feature vector derived therefrom, as input and to output a predicted directional transfer function or a set of interpolation weights for combining the plurality of stored directional transfer functions.

---

*[End of Claim Set]*

# Patent Claim

**Claim 1.** A method for processing audio signals, the method comprising:

obtaining a plurality of ambient signals representative of sound captured from an environment surrounding a listener;

applying a first directional transfer function pair to a first ambient signal of the plurality of ambient signals to generate a first spatially-processed signal, the first directional transfer function pair corresponding to a first direction relative to the listener;

applying a second directional transfer function pair to a second ambient signal of the plurality of ambient signals to generate a second spatially-processed signal, the second directional transfer function pair corresponding to a second direction relative to the listener, the second direction being different from the first direction;

combining the first spatially-processed signal and the second spatially-processed signal to generate an output audio signal for presentation to the listener;

detecting a directional change associated with at least one of the listener or a sound source associated with the plurality of ambient signals;

in response to detecting the directional change, selecting an updated directional transfer function pair from a plurality of stored directional transfer function pairs, the updated directional transfer function pair corresponding to an updated direction based on the detected directional change; and

applying the updated directional transfer function pair to at least one of the plurality of ambient signals to generate an updated spatially-processed signal reflecting the detected directional change.

---

**Claim 2.** The method of claim 1, wherein detecting the directional change comprises receiving head-tracking data indicative of a rotation or movement of the listener's head.

**Claim 3.** The method of claim 1, wherein detecting the directional change comprises analyzing the plurality of ambient signals to identify a shift in an estimated direction of arrival of a dominant sound source.

**Claim 4.** The method of claim 1, wherein each of the first directional transfer function pair and the second directional transfer function pair comprises a pair of head-related transfer functions (HRTFs) corresponding to a left-ear response and a right-ear response for a respective direction.

**Claim 5.** The method of claim 1, wherein selecting the updated directional transfer function pair comprises interpolating between two or more stored directional transfer function pairs based on the updated direction.

**Claim 6.** The method of claim 1, further comprising storing the plurality of stored directional transfer function pairs in a memory, each stored directional transfer function pair being associated with a discrete direction relative to the listener.

**Claim 7.** The method of claim 1, wherein combining the first spatially-processed signal and the second spatially-processed signal comprises summing the first spatially-processed signal and the second spatially-processed signal in a time domain or a frequency domain.

**Claim 8.** The method of claim 1, wherein the plurality of ambient signals are obtained from a plurality of microphones arranged on a wearable audio device.

**Claim 9.** The method of claim 1, further comprising, after applying the updated directional transfer function pair, cross-fading between the first spatially-processed signal and the updated spatially-processed signal to reduce an audible artifact associated with the directional change.

**Claim 10.** A non-transitory computer-readable medium storing instructions that, when executed by one or more processors, cause the one or more processors to perform the method of claim 1.

**Claim 11.** A system for processing audio signals, the system comprising:

a plurality of microphones configured to capture a plurality of ambient signals from an environment surrounding a listener;

one or more processors operatively coupled to the plurality of microphones and configured to:

apply a first directional transfer function pair to a first ambient signal of the plurality of ambient signals to generate a first spatially-processed signal, the first directional transfer function pair corresponding to a first direction relative to the listener;

apply a second directional transfer function pair to a second ambient signal of the plurality of ambient signals to generate a second spatially-processed signal, the second directional transfer function pair corresponding to a second direction relative to the listener, the second direction being different from the first direction;

combine the first spatially-processed signal and the second spatially-processed signal to generate an output audio signal;

detect a directional change associated with at least one of the listener or a sound source associated with the plurality of ambient signals; and

in response to detecting the directional change, select an updated directional transfer function pair from a plurality of stored directional transfer function pairs and apply the updated directional transfer function pair to at least one of the plurality of ambient signals to generate an updated spatially-processed signal; and

an output transducer configured to present the output audio signal to the listener.
